    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Loop it against the " },
        { "text": "Metronome", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/metronome" } },
        { "text": ", one chord per bar, slow enough that you're never rushing a change." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Then a second pass: before each change, find the note that doesn't move and put that finger down first. It's already home — the rest of the hand just settles around it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Then a third pass, to prove none of this is a fixed set of positions: start from C root position, "
        },
        { "text": "x x x 5 5 3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and go up instead of down. F's second inversion sits at " },
        { "text": "x x x 5 6 5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and G's at " },
        { "text": "x x x 7 8 7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", so the same three chords run frets 3 through 8 just as comfortably." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "tip",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Pick the copy of the next chord that's nearest your hand, and let a finger stay where it can. That's the whole trick."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 3 is next: the same twelve grips, with one note moved a single fret — and that note is the third, which you already know how to find in any of them."
        }
      ]
    }
